  "methodology": "LAND's portfolio is mapped from the FY2025 10-K Schedule III \"Real Estate and Accumulated Depreciation\" table, which lists 67 line items aggregating the company's 144 owned farms (some counties bundle multiple farms into one Schedule III row). Total cost basis $1,297M and net book $1,099M reconcile exactly to the published schedule. Per-property acres are not disclosed in Schedule III; instead, the 10-K Item 2 properties summary provides state-level farm-acre totals (e.g., CA 32,321; FL 8,635; CO 24,513). Per-line-item acres in the table below are pro-rata allocated within each state by net book value.\n\nFMV is built bottoms-up by mapping LAND's 98,688 farm acres to thirteen crop / region buckets, applying per-acre values sourced from the USDA NASS 2024 Land Values Summary, recent third-party appraisals, and comparable transactions reported by regional ag-real-estate brokers. The largest single value driver is California: 18K acres of mature permanent crop ground (almonds / pistachios / citrus / avocado) at ~$28K/acre, plus 4.5K acres of tier-1 coastal berry/vegetable ground at ~$55K/acre. Implied NAV/share at FMV deducts net debt (long-term debt + Series D Term Pref \u2212 cash) and divides by current diluted shares outstanding. The result is a sum-of-the-parts estimate, not a single appraisal \u2014 California permanent and coastal ground drives most of the gap to book, since LAND acquired these farms 5-10 years ago at materially lower per-acre values."
